A||N||7||Dan87GT||Drilling-out exhaust manifold bolt holes-how to drill||||1||
Z||000000||Dan87GT||06-25-2001||07:25 AM||||I am re-fixing my exhaust leak on my rear exhaust manifold. 2 of the top bolt holes are stripped. I am going to insert helicoils but have no idea how to get in there with a drill. How has everybody done this??||||reg||1
Z||000001||Toddster||06-25-2001||01:25 PM||||Dan you need to swing the engine cradle out of the way. Remove your rear brake line hold down bolts (the one that attach the lines to the frame behind each tire), remove the mud flaps from the cradle, remove the two rear bolts, with a jack under the frame and the car on jack stands, and slowly let the cradle drop to hang on the dog bone. Then climb into the trunk and you should be able to get at those bolt holes from above. If you need more room then remove the dog bone, unbolt the strut tower bolts, remove your transmission cables and the cradle can swing as much as 45 degrees! all the hoses rotate with it, no problem. Just be sure to keep the cradle supported.<P><BR>Its a good idea to remove the deck lid too. Just remove the 4 side mounted bolts. Be sure to use a scribe to mark the hinge placement so you replace the decklid in the same spot as before.<P>Good luck.<p>[This message has been edited by Toddster (edited 06-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Dan87GT||06-25-2001||02:35 PM||||Thanks, but when I said rear manifold I meant the one against the firewall, closest to the driver, also known as the fun one.. Any other thoughts???||||reg||1
Z||000003||sjp777||06-25-2001||02:56 PM||||Jack car way up, remove exhaust, ac compressor and go at it from the bottom. A right angle drill might help. I used an straight air drill 'cause it was the smallest I could find.<P>Be sure to wear saftey glasses, debris will be raining down on your face.<P>It totally sucks, but it can be done without dropping the engine or cradle.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_4.jpg"> <BR>88GT 5sp, in desperate need of an LT1<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||shiden_kai||06-25-2001||08:47 PM||||One of the previous posters had the right idea, you still need to swing the cradle<BR>and engine down, just at the front of the cradle, not the rear.  As long as you support the cradle as you lower it, it will come down far enough so that you can get a straight run at the exhaust bolt holes in the cylinder head.  When I did the original campaigns on these vehicles, which included repairing any broken bolts the the cylinder heads, I would swing the cradle down in the front side, repair that side of the engine, then swing down the rear side and repair<BR>those broken bolts.||||reg||1
Z||000005||StuGood||06-26-2001||10:48 PM||||He who has done plenty recalls, has better advice than me...  but I'll offer what I did.<P>1) Used an air drill, and chucked the drills about as far into the chuck as they'd go - to shorten the drill+ bit.<P>2) Lowered the aft end of the cradle, and attacked from above.  That doesn't mean that it's the easiest way - but it is possible to drill from above, while standing in the trunk.<P>3) I marked the hole center using a spring-loaded center punch, and a dental mirror (mirror on a stick) to see the end of the broken fastener.  Used the mirror several times as I drilled, to check how things were going.<P>4) Apply lotsa lube - squirted Liquid wrench onto the bolt and/ into the bolt after the center hole was all the way through the bolt.  Figured it can't hurt.<P>5) Apply lotsa patience! ||||reg||1
Z||000006||cadaver||06-27-2001||12:28 AM||||I have a killer right angle aircraft drill. It get those bolts without dropping anything.<BR>I have a right angle and a 45||||reg||1
Z||000007||william_thorniii||06-27-2001||05:18 PM||||shiden_kai - <P>How in the hell do you drop the front of the cradle and rotate it?  The front bolts are pivot bolts (mounted horizontally) and the cradle rotates about them.  The rear bolts are location bolts (mounted vertically).  The cradle will not rotate about them.  Are you suggesting removing the bolts completely and dropping the engine vertically out of the bay?  <P>William T. Thorn, III <BR>||||reg||1
